# (#126) THE ALL FALLBACK, arm 2: a builtin that runs interpreted code.
#
# `eval` compiles a NEW unit at run time from a string that need not exist
# until the moment it runs, so the census cannot see which names that unit
# reads. The opaque set is MIRRORED from the VM's own observer gate
# (chunk.c const_pool_names_observer names `eval` for exactly this reason,
# with record_history / classify / state_at / get_observer_thresholds
# beside it) rather than re-derived here.
#
# `x` is the read that makes the unit observed. `c` and `n` are named by
# NOTHING the static walk can see -- only by the eval'd strings -- so under
# a census WITHOUT this fallback they would compile to plain C statics and
# the eval'd `report of c` would read an empty slot (measured against a
# census with `eval` removed from OBS_OPAQUE_BUILTINS: `equilibrium` where
# the VM says `improving`). With the fallback the whole unit keeps today's
# bookkeeping, which is exactly the pre-#126 behaviour.
x is 100.0
c is 0.0
n is 0
loop while n < 6:
    x is x * 0.5
    c is c + 2.0
    n is n + 1
print of f"x {x} c {c} n {n}"
print of f"x-report {report of x}"
print of (eval of "report of c")
print of (eval of "report of n")
print of (eval of "report of x")
